    "final_answer": "I have successfully determined the convergence of the series using WolframAlpha. Here is the complete answer:\n\n## Result: The Series **CONVERGES**\n\n**Series:** Σ (n=1 to ∞) 1/(n³ + 1)\n\n**Sum value:** ≈ 0.68650 (essentially 0.686503...)\n\n### Convergence Test Results (from WolframAlpha):\n\n| Convergence Test (收敛检验) | Result (结果) |\n|---|---|\n| **Integral Test** (积分) | **Series Converges** (级数收敛) ✓ |\n| **Ratio Test** (比值) | Inconclusive (不确定) |\n| **Root Test** (根值) | Inconclusive (不确定) |\n\n### Mathematical Justification:\n\nThe **Integral Test** confirms convergence because the function f(x) = 1/(x³ + 1) is positive, continuous, and decreasing for x ≥ 1. Comparing it to the convergent p-series:\n\n$$\\frac{1}{n^3 + 1} < \\frac{1}{n^3}$$\n\nSince Σ 1/n³ converges (p-series with p = 3 > 1), by the Direct Comparison Test, the original series also converges.\n\nThe exact closed form involves the polygamma function:\n\n$$\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{n^3+1} = -\\frac{1}{3}\\sum_{\\{\\omega: \\omega^3+3\\omega^2+3\\omega+2=0\\}} \\frac{\\psi^{(0)}(-\\omega)}{(\\omega+1)^2} \\approx 0.68650$$",
